CHAPTER XII
INVESTIGATION
Article 21
(1) Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regional Government are given special authority as Investigators to conduct investigations of criminal acts, as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Law.
(2) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) are certain civil servant officials within the Regional Government who are appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) The authority of Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) is:
a. to receive, search for, collect, and examine information or reports concerning criminal acts so that such information or reports become more complete and clear;
b. to examine, search for, and collect information regarding individuals or Legal Entities about the truth of acts committed in connection with criminal acts;
c. to request information and evidence from individuals or Legal Entities in connection with criminal acts;
d. to examine books, records, and other documents concerning criminal acts;
e. to conduct searches to obtain evidence of bookkeeping, records, and other documents, as well as to carry out seizures of such evidence;
f. to request the assistance of experts in the framework of carrying out the duties of investigating criminal acts;
g. to order to stop and/or prohibit a person from leaving a room or place when an examination is in progress and to examine the identity of persons, objects, and/or documents being carried;
h. to photograph a person related to a criminal act;
i. to summon a person to be heard for their statement and examined as a suspect or witness;
j. to terminate an investigation; and/or
k. to take other actions necessary for the smooth conduct of investigation of criminal acts in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(4) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall notify the commencement of an investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 12
The implementation of prevention, protection and recovery for victims of acts of violence is carried out with:
a. Fast, safe, empathetic;
b. Guarantee of confidentiality;
c. Easily accessible; and
d. No fees charged.

CHAPTER III
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 4
Every victim has the right to receive:
a. Protection;
b. Information;
c. Minimum services in accordance with Minimum Service Standards (SPM);
d. Continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. Confidential handling;
f. Psychological and legal assistance; and
g. Guarantee of rights related to status as a family member as well as a member of society.

NO : 18/LD/2012
REGIONAL GAZETTE OF INDRAMAYU REGENCY NUMBER : 18 YEAR 2012
REGIONAL REGULATION OF INDRAMAYU REGENCY NUMBER : 18 YEAR 2012
CONCERNING
PREVENTION, PROTECTION AND RECOVERY OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN
AS VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE IN INDRAMAYU REGENCY
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD THE REGENT OF INDRAMAYU,

Considering : a. that violence against women and children is an act that violates the rights and dignity of women and children as human beings;
b. that the implementation of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children as victims of violence in Indramayu Regency has not been carried out optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b above, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Prevention, Protection and Recovery of Women and Children as Victims of Acts of Violence in Indramayu Regency.

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by Amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 169, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended for the second time by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
10. Government Regulation Number 41 of 2007 concerning Regional Apparatus Organization (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 89, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4741);
11. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
12. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 7 of 2008 concerning Regional Secretariat and DPRD Secretariat of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 7 Series D.3);
13. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 8 of 2008 concerning Regional Agencies of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 8 Series : D 4);
14. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 9 of 2008 concerning Regional Technical Institutions and Civil Service Police Unit of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 9 Series D.5) as amended for the second time by Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 16 of 2012 concerning the Second Amendment to Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 9 of 2008 concerning Regional Technical Institutions and Civil Service Police Unit of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2012 Number 16).
15. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 5 of 2012 concerning Procedures for Establishing Regional Legal Products of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Number 5 Year 2012).

With Mutual Agreement

CHAPTER IX
REPORTING
Article 18
(1) P2TP2A is obligated to report the implementation of the organization of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children victims of acts of violence to the Regent.
(2) Reporting as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. Administration;
b. Finance;
c. Services;
d. Performance.
(3) Submission of written reports as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out at least once every 3 (three) months.

Article 12
The implementation of prevention, protection and recovery for victims of acts of violence is carried out with:
a. Fast, safe, empathetic;
b. Guarantee of confidentiality;
c. Easily accessible; and
d. No fees charged.

Article 16
Guidance, control and supervision as referred to in Chapter VII are directed to:
a. increase the level of public awareness so as to be able to prevent, protect and/or restore the physical and or psychological condition of women and children victims of acts of violence;
b. fulfill the community's need for information and protection services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
c. provide facilities in order to support the improvement of efforts and activities in the implementation of prevention, protection, recovery of women and children victims of acts of violence;
d. improve the quality of health for women and children who experience acts of violence.

